ithout changing the actual Python code.\n- Explain what a variable is before explaining its assignment.\n- Explain operators such as +=, >=, and < separately.\n- Break each loop iteration into smaller steps.\n- Explain why a line runs and what happens next.\n- Add a simple \"Why am I learning this?\" explanation when useful.\nExample: Instead of jumping from c += nums[i] to the next condition, explain the current number, the calculation, the new value of c, and then the next condition.\n7.  Phase 2 — Animate variables and calculations\nMake invisible operations visible\n- Show a variable as a labeled box containing its current value.\n- Animate a number moving into a variable when assigned.\n- Show arithmetic calculations step by step.\n- Animate a variable changing from its previous value to its new value.\n- Visually show when a negative value causes c to reset to zero.\nKeep animations short, optional, and easy to follow.\n\n\n\n\nPhase 3 — Visualize conditions and loops\nExplain decisions clearly\n- Show comparisons using actual values, such as -2 >= -2.\n- Display a clear True or False result.\n- Highlight the branch that executes.\n- Show the loop's current index and current list element.\n- Visually return to the loop when another iteration begins.\n8.  Phase 4 — Add interactive learning\nLet learners predict what happens\n\nBefore selected steps, ask questions such as:\n- What will c become after this addition?\n- Will this condition be True or False?\n- Will ans change?\nLet the learner answer, then reveal the correct result and explanation. Make this feature optional.\n9. what is this nothing Is vissible, we better to remove this phase i thing , tell me th ebrutal truth.\n10. Phase 5 — Test and polish\nProtect existing functionality\n- Verify that Previous Step and Next Step restore correct values.\n- Check every iteration and condition.\n- Test all-negative, positive, and single-element inputs.\n- Add animation speed controls and a reduced-motion option.\n- Confirm the application still works without API calls.\n\n<summary>\n1. **Task Overview**\n   - **Core Request**: Implement **Phase 5 — Test and polish** for the offline \"Teach Me This Code\" Code Flow Visualizer located in `C:\\Users\\CVT\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\code-flow-visualizer\\app_build\\`:\n     1. Verify that `Previous Step` and `Next Step` restore correct runtime values across snapshots.\n     2. Check every iteration and condition (True/False branch outcomes, loop indices, returns).\n     3. Test all-negative, positive, and single-element inputs (`[-5, -2, -8, -1, -4]`, `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]`, `[-7]`, `[42]`).\n     4. Add animation speed controls (`0.5x`, `1x`, `1.5x`, `2x`) and a reduced-motion option (WCAG compliance with `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` and manual toggle).\n     5. Confirm the application still works with 100% offline local execution without API calls.\n   - **Constraints**:\n     - Zero AI APIs, external cloud dependencies, or paid libraries.\n     - Do not rebuild the application from scratch; preserve the foundational execution engine (`engine.js`), explanation rules (`explainer.js`), and existing UI design.\n     - Provide GitHub-style markdown responses with clickable `file:///` links.\n     - Avoid executing terminal commands via `run_command` in this workspace if they wait on manual user confirmation.\n\n2. **Progress**\n   - **Completed in Phase 5**:\n     1. [app_build/index.html](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/index.html):\n        - Added `<button id=\"btnSpeed\" class=\"btn-subtle\" title=\"Adjust Auto Play & Animation Speed\">⚡ Speed: 1x</button>` in the top navigation `.secondary-actions`.\n        - Added `<div class=\"test-inputs-strip\">` directly below the dynamic overview strip with interactive test chips for:\n          - Mixed: `nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]` (Max: 6)\n          - All-Negative: `nums = [-5, -2, -8, -1, -4]` (Max: -1)\n     